We are extending our internal API gateway (Python 3.11, standard library only). Here is the current handler module:

```python
# gateway/handler.py
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass


@dataclass
class Request:
    client_id: str
    method: str
    path: str


class Gateway:
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.routes: dict[str, object] = {}

    def register(self, path: str, handler: object) -> None:
        self.routes[path] = handler

    def handle(self, request: Request) -> dict:
        handler = self.routes.get(request.path)
        if handler is None:
            return {"status": 404, "body": "not found"}
        return handler(request)
```

Add per-client rate limiting to the Gateway using the token bucket algorithm:

1. Each client_id gets its own bucket with a capacity of 20 tokens, refilled at 10 tokens per second.
2. Buckets are created lazily on first request and must not grow unbounded: evict buckets that have been idle for more than 60 seconds.
3. If a request arrives and its bucket has no tokens, return {"status": 429, "body": "rate limited", "retry_after": <seconds until the next token>} without calling the handler.
4. Use time.monotonic() for all timing so the limiter stays correct across wall-clock adjustments.
5. Keep the existing public API (register/handle) unchanged.

